{"term":"take up","senses":[{"id":"kaikki:811cacb2246cbb1ade","definition":"Alternative form of take-up.","example":"In America alone, people spent $170 billion on “direct marketing”—junk mail of both the physical and electronic varieties—last year. Yet of those who received unsolicited adverts through the post, only 3% bought anything as a result. If the bumf arrived electronically, the take-up rate was 0.1%. And for online adverts the “conversion” into sales was a minuscule 0.01%.","pos":"noun","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"alt-of, alternative","register":"alt-of, alternative, countable, uncountable","wordFamily":[],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/take_up#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:ca18fd7706d9c525ff","definition":"To lift; to raise.","example":"I say unto thee, Arise, and take up thy bed, and go thy way into thine house.","pos":"verb","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"transitive","wordFamily":[],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/take_up#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:9717e0c25068524807","definition":"To occupy; to consume (space or time).","example":"The books on finance take up three shelves.","pos":"verb","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"transitive","wordFamily":[],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/take_up#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:6f8b2013b1db963822","definition":"To take, to assume (one’s appointed or intended place).","example":"She took up her post at the foot of the stairs.","pos":"verb","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"transitive","wordFamily":[],"source":"En